window.docContents[99].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Lower-Order-Divergence-Form-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems","header":"5.4.2.5. Lower-Order Divergence-Form Problems","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Lower-Order-Divergence-Form-Problems"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Coverage-conventions":{"contents":"The entries distinguish three kinds of coverage. A topic\nis formalized when PDEAtlas states and proves it, inherited when the\nBlueprint cites the Mathlib declarations directly, without local restatements,\nand partial when the reusable core is formalized in a stated range or\ngeometric setting; each partial entry names that range.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.1. Coverage conventions","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Coverage-conventions"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Contact-Preservation-and-the-Local-Solution":{"contents":"The contact identity says that the differential of the transported value is\nrepresented by the transported momentum. Once it is preserved along the\nflow, inverse characteristic coordinates define the spatial function\n and identify its gradient with .\n\nA regular local flow box consists of  maps  satisfyingtheir prescribed initial traces, and the homogeneous first-variation equation\nfor the contact defect\n. A uniform local bound on  records\nthe coefficient control used by ODE uniqueness. The structure collects\nhypotheses; a constructor builds one from a  characteristic family.\n\nThe contact equation follows by differentiating the defect in time. The\nboundary derivative supplied by the ODE construction satisfies the\nvariational equation, so this step needs only a  family. Differentiating\nthe preserved residual  in a boundary direction cancels the remaining\nterms and gives .\n\nFor a globally  Hamiltonian, a  characteristic family with the\nprescribed initial trace, compatible initial gradients, and a local bound on\n determines a regular flow box, provided its boundary derivative\nsatisfies the variational equation. The constructor derives the component\nequations and the contact evolution from these hypotheses.\n\nThe contact defect measures the failure of  to represent the differential\nof  in the boundary directions. Compatibility makes it vanish at ,\nand its homogeneous linear evolution forces it to vanish for all\ncharacteristic times; this is the step that turns the transported momentum\ninto a gradient.\n\nFor a regular characteristic flow satisfying the first-variation equation,\nthe contact defectvanishes initially by compatibility and remains zero by uniqueness for its\nhomogeneous linear ODE. Combining the tangential and characteristic-time\ndirections proves that the differential of  is the pullback of the\ncovector represented by .\n\nCompatibility makes the contact defect zero at initial time. Its\nfirst-variation equation is a homogeneous scalar linear ODE with locally\nbounded coefficient, so uniqueness forces it to remain zero. Together with\nthe characteristic-time identity, this identifies every derivative of \nwith pairing against  through the derivative of .\n\nEvans's construction combines three ingredients: inverse\ncharacteristic coordinates from the noncharacteristic condition,  from\ncontact preservation, and  from constraint preservation.\n\nFor a  phase-space Hamiltonian, given compatible boundary gradients,\na noncharacteristic basepoint, and a regular characteristic flow, there are\nopen neighborhoods of the boundary parameter and boundary point and a \nfunction  that satisfieson the spatial neighborhood and has the prescribed boundary trace. Local\ninvertibility, , and the PDE residual are all derived from\nnoncharacteristicity and the characteristic and first-variation equations.\n\nInvert the spatial characteristic map near the noncharacteristic basepoint\nand define . Contact preservation and the inverse-map\nidentity give ; residual preservation then yields\n. Restricting to the initial surface recovers the prescribed\ntrace. The momentum in inverse coordinates is , so  is \nand  is .\n\nThe local existence theorem takes a flow box as input. To construct it from\nboundary data, first solve the compatibility equations for the momentum by the\nimplicit function theorem;  boundary and value data give a \nmomentum field. The  flow and its variational equation then provide the\ncontact transport needed for the local  solution.\n\nAssume  is  near the initial phase , the\nboundary parametrization  and values  are  on an open parameter\nneighborhood of , and the base momentum  satisfiesSuppose the initial tangent/characteristic map at this state is invertible,\nand  lies in the open ambient domain. Then some open spatial\nneighborhood within that domain carries a  classical solution of\n with trace  near  and .\n\nChoose a globally  Hamiltonian  agreeing with  near the initial\nphase. Its value and derivatives there preserve compatibility and\nnoncharacteristicity. For , the implicit function theorem constructs a\ncompatible  momentum field through . Construct the \ncharacteristic family and its first variation. Shrink the flow box\nso its positions lie in the ambient domain and  is bounded. Residual\npreservation and the variational equation give contact transport; the\nnoncharacteristic map is the initial position derivative. The preceding\ntheorem supplies inverse coordinates and the local  solution, with\nbase gradient . Continuity of  permits a smaller spatial\nneighborhood on which . Restrict the boundary\nneighborhood as well to retain the trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Nonlinear First-Order Equations\u0009Characteristics and Local Existence","header":"7.4.1.5. Contact Preservation and the Local Solution","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Contact-Preservation-and-the-Local-Solution"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-classical-wave-from-bounded-scalar-data":{"contents":"Consider  with displacement  and\nvelocity . The reaction is globally Lipschitz and the\ninitial profiles are bounded and smooth. Their derivatives need no uniform\nbound, and  is allowed. In one dimension the characteristic\nintegral constructs a classical solution from these data for every\nnonnegative time.\n\nLet  be the free d'Alembert wave with data . The same\nzero-data Duhamel operator used in the linear chapter takes, for ,\nthe form\n\n\n\nThe nonlinear equation  asks us to solve\n. On a finite strip ,\nbounded continuous fields form a complete space in the supremum norm.\nContinuity here is joint in time and space. The estimate\n keeps the source bounded, and\n keeps its Duhamel field bounded.\n\nFor bounded continuous , a globally\n-Lipschitz , and every finite , there is a unique\nbounded continuous solution  of  on\n. If , restricting  to\n gives .\n\nWrite . Its Lipschitz constant is at most\n, which need not be less than one. Repeated time integration\ngives the stronger estimateFor each fixed , the coefficient tends to zero as .\nSome iterate therefore contracts, giving a unique fixed point of\n on that whole strip. There is no small-time restriction on\n. The value at time  uses source values only between  and\n, so restriction to a shorter strip still solves its integral\nequation. Uniqueness identifies the restricted solution.\n\nThe integral solution is initially only continuous. To get two derivatives,\nwe insert it into the Duhamel formula twice. Extend  by clipping\nonly its time argument to , and call this continuous extension\n. Define\n\n\n\nWith  and , the free field  is .\nThe one-dimensional Duhamel operator sends continuous forcing to a jointly\n field, so  is . If , its composition\nwith  is , and the linear Duhamel regularity theorem makes\n jointly . Causality identifies both insertions with\n on the closed forward strip. This proves regularity of the actual\nfixed point from ordinary scalar regularity of the data and reaction.\n\nLet  be  and globally Lipschitz,\nand let  and  have\nbounded ranges. There is a field  such thatBoth prescribed values are joint spacetime traces:For every finite , the field is bounded on\n; the bound may depend on .\nNo integrability, smallness, vanishing condition on , or bound on\nthe derivatives of  is assumed.\n\nThe compatible strip solutions define one field for all forward times.\nNear any positive time, choose a fixed larger horizon. Compatibility makes\nthe field agree on that neighborhood with the  second insertion\nfor this horizon. Near time zero, use the signed-time extension from the\nunit strip. These local representations prove global  regularity;\nthe nonlinear PDE is asserted for nonnegative time.The Duhamel field has zero initial displacement and velocity, so subtracting\nit from  retains both assigned initial values. Continuity of \nand  then gives the two joint traces. On every finite forward\nstrip the field agrees with the bounded continuous fixed point, giving the\nstated horizon-dependent bound.\n\nThe uniqueness assertion in this construction concerns bounded continuous\nsolutions of the integral equation on each finite strip. The raw classical\nendpoint above states existence; comparisons between general classical\nfields use the energy and cone arguments below. Global forward existence\nalso allows growth in time: for a constant reaction  and\nzero initial data, the constructed field is  for\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations","header":"7.2.4.3. A classical wave from bounded scalar data","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-classical-wave-from-bounded-scalar-data"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl--Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl":{"contents":"\n\nAn axisymmetric velocity has three cylindrical components ,\nall functions of . For smooth Cartesian axisymmetric fields, the\nswirl  and azimuthal vorticity  vanish on the\naxis. Away from the axis, Hou and Li (Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 61, 2008) evolve\n\n\n\nwhere  is the Stokes streamfunction. The meridional velocity is then\n and , and the\nthree reduced equations use the operator\n, which is the Laplacian of a\nradial function in four transverse variables. The formulation goes back to\n.\n\nOff the axis, the reduced Navier–Stokes system for\n with viscosity  isThe source terms describe stretching and vorticity production:\nswirl is stretched by axial variation of the streamfunction, and the\ncentrifugal term  creates azimuthal vorticity. Lean records\neach equation as a pointwise residual in the meridian coordinates, and\nthe reduced system asks all three to vanish on a chosen domain and time\nset.\n\nSmoothness of the reduced profiles at the axis needs more than vanishing\nof the numerators there. We instead start with smooth functions of \nand set  and . The resulting Cartesian\nformulas contain no division by the radius.\n\nFor profiles  of  and  with\n, the reconstructed velocity is the sum ofwith every profile evaluated at . In cylindrical components these are\n,  and .\n\n\n\nThe simplest profile,  with no swirl, gives the strain flow\n of the figure: a material cylinder narrows and lengthens,\nand its volume  stays fixed because the radial factor in the\nmeridian measure compensates for the loss of meridional area.\n\nIf  is  and  is  on the whole -plane, the\nreconstructed velocity is divergence free on all of , the axis\nincluded. It is equivariant under rotations about the -axis, and on the\naxis its first two components vanish. Smooth profiles give a smooth velocity.\n\nThe swirl part is divergence free because \nequals . For the meridional part, the chain rule\nthrough  giveswhich cancel. Equivariance follows because  and  are rotation\ninvariant while  and  rotate with the point.\n\nFor  swirl and  streamfunction profiles,The vorticity of the swirl has the same shape as the meridional velocity of a\nstreamfunction, with  in place of . The azimuthal part is\n, so , and  is the operator  of the\nreduced system rewritten in the variable .\n\nThe curl is linear, so the meridional and swirl parts are treated separately.\nEach component is a product of a coordinate with a lifted profile; the chain\nrule for  produces the factors  and , and collecting terms\ngives the displayed coefficients.\n\nThe evolution equations are best stated in spacetime profiles \nof . With the transport operator\n\n\n\nand the squared-radius form  of\n, the swirl and vorticity equations read\n and .\n\nThe squared-radius residuals areand  gives the Euler versions. The vorticity reconstructed from the\ncoefficients isin which  is a separate unknown. It becomes the curl of the velocity\nonly once the stream equation  holds.\n\nIf  are smooth and , the Cartesian velocity\n and the reconstructed vorticity  satisfy the three-dimensional\nvorticity equationeverywhere in spacetime. For Euler,  swirl and streamfunction and a\n vorticity coefficient suffice. Local versions hold on an open set of\nprofile arguments, where the profiles need not extend past its boundary.\n\nEach Cartesian component of the transport residual is computed explicitly and\nturns out to be a combination of the lifted reduced residuals with polynomial\ncoefficients in . The stretching term  supplies the\nsources  and , and the lifted viscous term reproduces\n. When both reduced residuals vanish, every component vanishes.\n\nLet  be smooth on an open set  of profile arguments and\nsatisfy ,  and  there. On every open\nconvex spatial set  and every time set  whose points map into ,\nthere is a pressure  withThe pressure is chosen independently on each time slice.\n\nThe stream equation identifies  with  near each point\nof , after replacing the profiles by compactly supported smooth ones that\nagree nearby. The vorticity equation then says that the vector field\n has zero curl on each slice. On a convex open set a\ncurl-free smooth field is a gradient, which is the Navier–Stokes pressure\ntheorem for the vorticity form.\n\nLet  be continuous and  be , and write\n, . If\n, ,  and\n are integrable for the meridian measure\n, then  and\n\nIn cylindrical coordinates . Expanding the last square gives\n. Against the\nweight , the first two terms are , which\nintegrates to zero on almost every axial line because the moment of\n is finite. The cylindrical change of variables then gives the\nfactor .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Axisymmetric Flow with Swirl","header":"8.6.1. Axisymmetric Flow with Swirl","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl--Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Maximum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness":{"contents":"Parabolic equations propagate information forward from the initial face and\ninward from the spatial boundary. Their natural boundary is therefore not the\nentire boundary of a spacetime cylinder: the spatial interior of the\nterminal-time face is excluded, while its lateral boundary points remain.\nThe parabolic boundary encodes this causal geometry.\n\nThe maximum principle rules out a new positive maximum in the positive-time\ninterior. A small perturbation by  makes the differential\ninequality strict, compactness supplies a maximizer, and the spatial\nsecond-derivative test conflicts with the time derivative at that point.\nApplying the same result to differences yields comparison and uniqueness.\n\nFor an open spatial domain  and , the closed spacetime\ncylinder is .\n\nBoundary data are prescribed on only part of the boundary of this cylinder.\nThe maximum-principle argument finds a maximizer over the whole closed set\nand must then be able to exclude it from the region where the differential\ninequality holds, and that region is , terminal face\nincluded.\n\nThe parabolic boundary consists of the initial face\n and the lateral boundary\n. The terminal face\n is excluded.\n\nThe operator to which the argument applies need not be the Laplacian. What\nthe second-derivative test uses at a spatial maximum is only that each\nprincipal term is a second derivative in a fixed direction. The directions\nmay vary with the point, but they are held fixed while differentiating the\nspatial slice; no derivatives of the coefficient fields enter.\n\nFor a scalar field  on a real normed space, finitely many direction\nfields , and a drift , defineThe potential variant is . The definitions impose\nno sign condition on ; \nassumes .Writing  for coordinate  of direction , the\nprincipal matrix has entries .\nEvery symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix has such a factorization. The theorems use\nonly this semidefinite sign.\n\nFor the heat equation itself the factorization is the standard basis:\n gives , and .\n\nFor a field written with separate spatial and time arguments, the classical\nheat operator is . The heat maximum, comparison, and\nuniqueness statements use this operator directly.\n\nThe first principle is stated for the factored operator without zeroth-order\nterm, and only the semidefinite sign of the principal part enters. Its\nhypotheses are the template for the other maximum principles:\ncontinuity on the closed cylinder, classical differentiability at each point\nof , the differential inequality there, and a bound on\nthe parabolic boundary.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, and let .\nAssume  is continuous on the closed cylinder. At every\n, assume its spatial slice is  at ,\nits time slice is differentiable at , and .\nIf  on the parabolic boundary, then\n throughout the closed cylinder.The time derivative is required at  even at spatial interior points,\nwhere terminal boundary data are not prescribed. It is the two-sided\nderivative on the real line, not a one-sided derivative within . The\npotential variants use the same hypotheses; the heat versions ask for less at\nthe terminal face.\n\nFor , the perturbation \nsatisfies  and retains the boundary upper bound.\nIf its maximum on the compact cylinder exceeded , a maximizer would\nlie in . There the spatial gradient vanishes and every\ndirectional second derivative is nonpositive. The time derivative is\nnonnegative, including at , where comparison with earlier times gives\nthe same sign. Thus , a contradiction. Let\n to recover the bound for .\n\nA zeroth-order term  with  does not disturb the argument\nprovided the bound  is nonnegative. At a point where  the\nterm  is nonnegative, so it only makes the inequality \nharder to satisfy. This is where the sign restriction on  enters.\n\nIf , , , and  on the\nparabolic boundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder. Taking\n to be the maximum of the positive part of the boundary data gives\nEvans's Theorem 9(i).\n\nThe same perturbation  produces the additional\nterm , which has the favorable sign because\n. At a positive maximum the zeroth-order term is also\nnonnegative.\n\nReplacing  by  turns subsolutions into supersolutions and upper\nbounds into lower bounds. The sign condition on  flips with it: the\nminimum principle requires , precisely so that  is an\nadmissible upper bound for .\n\nIf , , , and  on the\nparabolic boundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder. This is\nthe dual minimum statement in Evans's Theorem 9(ii), with the sign restriction\non  required by the nonnegative zeroth-order coefficient.\n\nApply the maximum principle with potential to  and the nonnegative upper\nbound , then negate the resulting inequality.\n\nFor the heat operator,  and the principal part is the full Laplacian,\nso the sign restriction on  disappears. The statement is repeated for\nthe heat operator so that no factorization of the\nidentity matrix has to be supplied when it is invoked. It is also sharper at\nthe top of the cylinder: derivatives and the differential inequality are\nneeded only on the open slab . Continuity carries the\nbound from every shorter cylinder up to the terminal face.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, , and \ncontinuous on the closed cylinder. Suppose that at every\n the spatial slice is , the time slice is\ndifferentiable, and . If  on the parabolic\nboundary, then  throughout the closed cylinder.\n\nAt a spatial maximum , while at a positive-time maximum on\n one has . The perturbation\n reduces the non-strict case to this strict\ncontradiction on each cylinder  with ,\nwhere the hypotheses hold up to the top face. Every point of\n lies in such a cylinder, and the closed cylinder is the\nclosure of this open slab, so continuity gives the bound everywhere.\n\nNegating a supersolution of the heat equation produces a subsolution with the\nnegated bound.\n\nFor the heat operator, if  on \nand  on the parabolic boundary, then  on the closed\ncylinder. Unlike the potential case, no sign condition on  is needed.\n\nSet . Then , while the boundary\nhypothesis  becomes . The weak maximum principle gives\n throughout the cylinder, which is equivalent to .\n\nApplied to a difference , the maximum principle compares two\nfunctions instead of bounding one.\n\nIf  on  and  on the\nparabolic boundary, with the classical regularity required by the maximum\nprinciple, then  on the entire closed cylinder.\n\nSet . Linearity of the heat operator gives\n, and the boundary assumption gives  on the\nparabolic boundary. The weak maximum principle therefore yields \non the closed cylinder, which is exactly .\n\nUniqueness is comparison applied twice.\n\nCompactness of  supplies the point where the perturbed\nsolution attains its maximum. On an unbounded domain, behavior at infinity\nneeds a separate argument.\n\nLet  be nonempty and open with compact closure, and . Under the\nregularity hypotheses of , two\nclassical heat solutions with equal residual on  and equal\nparabolic boundary data agree throughout the closed cylinder:\n\nThe two solutions have the same heat residual and the same parabolic boundary\ndata. Comparison first gives ; exchanging the two solutions gives\n. Pointwise antisymmetry of the real order then gives  on the\nwhole closed cylinder.\n\nOn all of  there is no compact cylinder, and uniqueness fails\nwithout some control at infinity: Tychonoff's classical example is a nonzero\nsolution with zero initial data that grows faster than any Gaussian. The\nremedy in  is a growth bound of the form\n. Any fixed Gaussian rate  is allowed, however\nlarge, and the time horizon  is arbitrary.\n\nLet  and . Let  be continuous on ,\nwith  spatial slices and differentiable time slices on\n, and suppose  there. If\n for , thenwith suprema in the extended reals. The same equality holds with \nreplaced by .\n\nOnly the upper bound needs work, and the extended-real formulation makes the\ncase of initial data unbounded above trivial. So fix  with\n. On a short time interval, of length fixed by ,\ncompare  on a large ball with  plus a small multiple of the growing\nGaussian . This barrier\nsolves the heat equation, and on the sphere  it outgrows\n once  is large. The weak maximum principle on the ball\ngives  plus the small multiple, and letting the multiple tend\nto zero gives . The short intervals are then concatenated up\nto . The equality with the positive-time supremum uses continuity at\n.\n\nThe growth bound is needed only for , and the equation only at\ninterior times.\n\nLet  and  satisfy the regularity hypotheses of\n and solve the same\nforced equation  on .\nIf  and  for ,\nand , then  on .\n\nThe difference  solves the homogeneous equation, vanishes at\n, and satisfies . The\nmaximum principle applied to  and to  gives  and\n.\n\nThe weak principle says the maximum is attained on the parabolic boundary. It\ndoes not rule out an interior maximum as well. The strong principle says that\nan interior maximum at a positive time  forces the solution to be\nconstant on the whole cylinder below . The statement is only about the\npast. Boundary values imposed after  can still change the solution\nthere.\n\nThe strong principle is proved for a general operator in nondivergence form.\nThe coefficients multiply second derivatives directly and are never\ndifferentiated.\n\nFor a matrix field , a drift , and a potential ,When  and the spatial slice is\n at the point, this agrees with the factored operator.\n\nLet  be open and connected. Let , ,  be continuous on\n, with  symmetric and\n for some . Let  be\ncontinuous on , with  spatial slices and\ndifferentiable time slices on . Suppose  there and\n attains its maximum over the closed cylinder at  with\n and . If , or if  and\n, then  on\n. The minimum versions hold for , with\n in the case .\n\nPut  and . The sign conditions ensure that\n is a supersolution. The argument proves that positivity of  moves\nforward in time. If , a small cubic bump centred at  lies\nbelow  at time . Moved along a segment and damped exponentially\nin time, the bump remains a subsolution: uniform ellipticity and bounds on\nthe continuous coefficients over a compact piece of the cylinder fix the\ndamping rate. Comparison on the moving ball then keeps  at the end of\nthe segment. Any two points of the connected set  are joined by a finite\nchain of overlapping balls inside . So if  for some ,\nthen , which is false. Hence  on ,\nand continuity extends this to .The proof uses only comparison with explicit barriers. It needs neither boundedness of\n nor derivatives of the coefficients. It also uses derivatives only\nstrictly before , so a maximum at  is covered.\n\nFor the heat equation,  is the identity matrix, , , and\n. The strong principle then holds for extrema of either sign.\n\nLet  be open and connected, and let  be continuous on\n, with  spatial slices and differentiable time\nslices on . If  and  attains its maximum over\nthe closed cylinder at an interior point  with ,\nthen  is constant on . The same holds for a\nminimum of a supersolution.\n\nThe heat operator equals the matrix operator with coefficients\n, identically in . The identity matrix is symmetric with\nellipticity constant one, and constant coefficients are continuous.\n\nThe heat case also follows from the mean value formula on heat balls\n(), a route specific to the\nLaplacian.\n\nA consequence is infinite speed of propagation on bounded domains. With zero\nboundary values, a nonnegative initial temperature that is positive\nsomewhere makes the temperature strictly positive everywhere inside at every\npositive time.\n\nLet  be open and connected with compact closure. Let  be a\nclassical supersolution,  on , with\n on the parabolic boundary and  for some\n. Then  for every  and\n.\n\nBy the weak minimum principle  on the closed cylinder. If\n for some  and , then  is an interior\nminimum, and the strong principle makes  identically zero on\n. This contradicts .\n\nOn the whole line the same fact is visible in the kernel formula\n(), since the Gaussian is positive. On a\nbounded interval, the figure computes the zero-boundary solution from its\nsine series.\n\n\n\nThe Harnack inequality is a quantitative form of the strong principle. It\ncompares a nonnegative solution at an earlier time with the same solution at\na later time, uniformly over a compact subregion. The constant depends on the\ngeometry and the coefficients. It is chosen before the solution.\n\nLet  be open, and let  be connected with compact closure\n. Let . Let , ,  be\ncontinuous on , with  symmetric and uniformly elliptic;\n may have either sign. Then there is  with the following\nproperty. Let  be continuous on . Suppose  has\ncontinuous spatial first and second derivatives and a continuous time\nderivative on , and  there. Then\n\nThe proof uses the contact-set method of Krylov and Safonov, in the form\npresented by Imbert and Silvestre. Suppose a supersolution is small at some\npoint at a later time. Slide a family of paraboloids up from below until\nthey first touch the graph at earlier times. At each contact point the\nspatial Hessian of the solution is bounded below by that of the paraboloid.\nThe supersolution inequality then bounds the Jacobian of the contact map,\nusing only ellipticity and bounds on the trace and the drift. The change of\nvariables formula turns this into a lower bound on the measure of the set\nwhere the solution was small earlier. An expanding barrier and an iteration\nof this growth step give a measure estimate on nested cylinders. The upper\nside comes from propagating almost-maximal values forward to later balls.\nTogether they give the comparison on small cylinders, and a finite covering\nof  gives the constant. A potential of either sign is\nabsorbed by adding one spatial variable  and multiplying by an\nexponential in . The potential then becomes a drift in . The\ncontact argument needs a jointly  gradient. The classical hypotheses\nstated here are reached by averaging forward in time and letting the width\ntend to zero.The coefficients are only assumed continuous.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow","header":"6.2.5. Maximum, Comparison, and Uniqueness","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Maximum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution":{"contents":"The explicit Gaussian formula uses constant coefficients and whole-space\ngeometry. To study  with a measurable, time-dependent operator field\n, we use the variational framework and its boundedness and coercivity\nhypotheses on a Gelfand triple, typically\n.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.3. Weak Evolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Evolution":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.8. Galerkin Construction for Weak Evolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction-for-Weak-Evolution"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Poisson___s-energy":{"contents":"Variational methods begin from the observation that some weak equations are\nstationarity conditions for an energy. For Poisson's equation with\nhomogeneous Dirichlet data, the energy is\n\n\n\nFor , take , so that\nperturbing  by  preserves the boundary condition.\nExpanding the square gives the exact identity\n\n\n\nAt a minimum, the coefficient of  must vanish: otherwise\nchoosing the opposite sign of a sufficiently small  lowers\nthe energy. That coefficient is the first variation,\n\n\n\nThus a minimizer is a weak solution, once differentiability along admissible\ndirections has been proved. Existence of that minimizer is the separate\nquestion that the direct method answers. The second variation then asks what\nkind of critical point has been found; for a quadratic energy built from a\nsymmetric bilinear form the Hessian is that form, so coercivity controls\nexistence, uniqueness, and stability at once.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points","header":"9.1. Poisson's energy","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Poisson___s-energy"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fractional-spaces-on-domains":{"contents":"Three domain constructions describe different ways to control a function\nnear the boundary.\n\n1. HsRestrictionSpace is the complete real Hilbert quotient of whole-space\n    by the kernel of restriction into .  It is available\n   at nonnegative order, carries the infimum-over-extensions quotient norm,\n   and has a canonical least-norm whole-space extension.\n   hsRestrictionSubmodule retains the corresponding raw-function membership\n   interface.2. GagliardoSpace is the complete real Hilbert graph space of the intrinsic\n   difference quotient on .\n   gagliardoSubmodule retains the corresponding raw-function membership\n   interface. Its intended Sobolev interpretation is .3. HsSupportedClosureSpace is the closed complex subspace of whole-space\n    generated by Schwartz functions supported in .  It is\n   complete for every real order, but its elements remain whole-space\n   distributions.\n\nFor  and , the completed whole-space Gagliardo model is\ncontinuously linearly equivalent to . The Fourier\nseminorm identity supplies the norm comparison, and the equivalence preserves\nthe underlying  function in both directions.\n\nFor any domain , the same comparison identifies the quotient of\nwhole-space Gagliardo functions by the restriction kernel with\nHsRestrictionSpace. Restricting a whole-space representative then gives a\nbounded injective map into the intrinsic GagliardoSpace on ,\npreserving the physical  class. Its range is exactly the intrinsic\nfunctions that admit a whole-space Gagliardo extension.\n\nFor a half-space, even reflection supplies the extension. It doubles the\nsquared  norm and increases the squared fractional seminorm by at most\na factor of four, so its operator norm is at most . Combining reflection\nwith the whole-space Fourier comparison gives a continuous linear\nequivalence between the intrinsic and restriction spaces, for every\n and . Both directions preserve the underlying\n class.\n\nOn a domain with a  extension atlas, in positive dimension and for\n, the reflected chart extensions and the interior zero extension glue\ninto a bounded intrinsic extension operator. The same argument then makes\nthe canonical map from the restriction space to the intrinsic Gagliardo space\na continuous linear equivalence. Both\ndirections preserve the physical  class, and the inverse norm\nis bounded by the whole-space comparison factor times the norm of the atlas\nextension.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.13. Fractional spaces on domains","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fractional-spaces-on-domains"}});